{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>But did not lie in wait, and the Elohim, etc.</b> One needs to know why the verse repeated ״לֹא צָדָה״ ״וְהָאֱלֹהִים״ וְגוֹ׳? And further it is difficult: why would HaShem bring this calamity upon a man? And our Sages of blessed memory said in the Mekhilta and in the Gemara (Makkot 10a): this is what the verse meant when it says ״מֵרְשָׁעִים יֵצֵא רֶשַׁע״ וְגוֹ׳ (I Samuel 24:14). How so? He was going down the ladder and it fell upon him and killed him, etc. — this one goes to exile; he was going up the ladder and it fell upon him and killed him — this one does not go to exile, etc.; one who came intentionally yet fell by the hand of an inadvertent [killer] — thus far. And it is difficult: it is true that this intentional [killer] received his punishment in full, for he was liable to death and he was indeed killed. But this inadvertent [killer] who goes to exile — this exile is for what he inadvertently did now on this occasion, but for his earlier inadvertence there is no payment for it. And if the occurrence that HaShem brought to his hand does not make him liable for it, since HaShem brought it to his hand — if so, one is forced to say that on this occasion HaShem did not bring it to his hand, and the one killed by his hand was not liable to death, for if he were liable, then this too would fall under the category of ״HaShem brought it to his hand,״ and this is far-fetched, for the verse describes the matter of inadvertent killing of a soul without intent (לֹא צְדִיָּה), and did not distinguish among any of them." "And the correct interpretation is that the first one killed was also liable to death, and his killing was by His word, blessed be He, Who brought it to the hand of this inadvertent killer. Now this one who was killed was caught in his transgression and was killed because he had killed intentionally — but HaShem could have brought a collapse upon him without the intermediary of an inadvertent killer. Rather, because there preceded it an inadvertent sin [lit. an intentional (inadvertent) sin] for which the only liability is exile, HaShem brought this matter to his hand. Now this man does not go to exile on account of the killing, but on account of what preceded it, which caused the killing. Accordingly, even if he killed ten people he is liable to only one exile, for the cause of the exile is one; and once he has paid for his sin, HaShem will not bring another killing to his hand. And you will find that HaShem did not fix one uniform term of exile for all killers, but rather each one according to the measure of the sin that preceded him. Accordingly, the reason the Talmud stated that this inadvertent killer's sin is for an earlier inadvertent killing aligns — and according to our words it is a hidden sin that preceded the first killing, or perhaps they adopted a common finding regarding the punishment of both of them, which is on account of the killing itself. And the one who understands the matter in his heart will grasp the foundation of these things." "<b>Or</b> it is possible that because they hinted to him from Heaven that through his hand the soul was killed — for the soul transmigrates (מִתְגַּלְגֵּל) through his hand — and he should have humbled himself and accepted exile upon himself of his own accord, but he added transgression upon his sin, therefore HaShem brought about yet another inadvertent [killing] beyond the first one, and he must go to exile twice, according to the measure required by the number of his wicked acts. And therefore you will find that HaShem in His wisdom did not fix a set term for the killer's exile but rather until the death of the High Priest — and this matter is entrusted to the Master of all the earth, and He will judge righteously how much measure is required for him, whether for one or for two. And according to this approach the intent of what it says will be settled, but still this is not sufficient to settle the verse's saying <b>וְהָאֱלֹהִים</b> with the additional ו." "<b>And it appears</b> that the verse intends to precise the law of exile — the manner in which one becomes liable to it — according to what Rambam wrote in chapter 6 of Hilkhot Rotze'ah: that one goes to exile only for an inadvertent [killing] that is not under duress — meaning, that the act is a commonplace thing, and that there was no aspect of intent at all. But if the inadvertence involved something unusual, or if there was an aspect of intent in it, such as an enemy whose presumption is intentional and similar cases, he does not go to exile. And corresponding to both of these the verse precise two things: <b>וַאֲשֶׁר</b> וְגוֹ׳ <b>וְהָאֱלֹהִים</b> וְגוֹ׳. Corresponding to inadvertence in something unusual it says <b>לֹא צָדָה</b> — meaning, he did not lie in wait to kill him, nor did he take precautions not to kill him, for every inadvertent killer has an aspect of liability, for he should have considered in his mind to prevent a mishap through his hand; but in a case where even if he had taken precautions it is something unusual — he is under duress (אָנוּס) and exempt. And corresponding to the aspect of intent in an enemy it says <b>וְהָאֱלֹהִים</b> וְגוֹ׳ — meaning, to exclude one in whom the matter came not from himself but from HaShem, for he had no desire to kill, since he is not his enemy, but rather the matter came from HaShem. But if there is also an aspect of hatred from him, that he was an enemy — he does not go to exile."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:32:03.587511333Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}